//Testing Service - Unit testing of service layer classes. They are used to test the business logic of the application. Testing of dependencies of these classes is more complicated, hence, we'll use the Repository pattern, which will simplify our testing. We don't need to mock the entity framework context each time though, as we can test the services using the built-in in-memory database support, provided by the visual studio 2012 and visual studio 2013.

//The contact ids are 1, 2, 3, etc., and the names are John Doe, Jane Doe, etc. So, for example, when we test the findById() method of our service implementation, we're going to pass in 1 and expect to receive a contact with id 1 and name John Doe.

//Testing Repository - Testing repository layer classes. They're used to test data access logic, often by mocking dependencies like the Entity Framework context. The idea here is to separate out your concerns, and unit test only the methods in the repository that pertain to data access, rather than the entire repository implementation. So in this case, we're just testing the findById() method. We would inject a mocked Entity Framework context into our repository and then call the method, passing in the id. We then validate that the correct SQL is generated and that the resultant contacts match what we’d expect.

//Testing Entity Framework Context - Testing the Entity Framework context is usually done in the integration testing phase, rather than unit testing, because it requires setting up and tearing down an actual database which can be slower and also can mess up your test database. So in this case, we'd setup a test database in our TestInitialize() method using an in-memory database then on TestCleanup we delete the test database. We'd also inject a mocked configuration into our context, so that we don’t have to manage the connection string which can increase our test speed.

//Testing Controller - This is where we make use of all the pieces to test the end to end operation of our application. We send a request to an action in our controller, validate that the correct actions are called, and validate some of the state of--------------------
            if (New_Ret >= 0) // If the specified object exists in the ArrayList
            {
                 Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);
            }
            else        // If the specified object does not exist in the ArrayList
            {
                 Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);

            }

            // Assert that the 'IndexOf' method returns -1 if the object is not found in the list
           Assert.IsTrue((New_Ret != -1) == New_arrListContainsX);

            // The order at which elements are added to a ArrayList doesn't guarantee that the last index is the highest.
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

Here is a list of the issues I am seeing:


*

*IndexOf: If the object is found in the ArrayList, it's behavior should be consistent with Contains (as both are O(1) methods). It doesn't appear that IndexOf works correctly when the object is found in the ArrayList. The test is failing for IndexOf because it is expecting a result, rather than returning -1 when it should be returning some index.

*LastIndexOf: Even though the current behavior is consistent with Contains, the test is still failing, it is supposed to return the same index if the object is found, not -1

*Count: If the object is found in the ArrayList, Count property should decrease 1, and remains constant if not found. However, the current behavior is only remaining constant.

*Contains: Contains returns false in its failure scenario, regardless of when you check it (whether at the initial assumption stage or later). 

*Add and Remove: The tests are successful for these methods.


I'm looking to solve these issues and test the remaining methods - Insert, RemoveAt, Clear, Contains, GetRange, Sort, TrimToSize, and TrimToSize

A: ArrayList class of .NET has been deprecated and replaced with System.Collections.Generic.List<T>. Pex should be able to handle the test of methods exposed by the List<T> class.
The general design of Pex is to test the public contracts of .NET Framework classes. This includes methods like IndexOf, Contains, Remove, etc., but not all methods of .NET Framework classes are currently supported by Pex. In general, methods with side-effects or internal implementation details might not be tested correctly by Pex. 
In your case, testing methods of ArrayList class in Pex might not be ideal, because ArrayList is not intended for that kind of testing. It would be better to refactor the code to use List<T> class or implement a similar class/data structure and then test its methods.
Checking if your contract tests are all working as expected might be difficult using the supplied Pex attributes just on ArrayList because ArrayList tests its attributes in a different way than .NET Collections. 
Please consider using a custom list class to make your testing easier and produce better results.
Check this microsoft discussion for more information: 
https://social.msdn.microsoft.com/Forums/vstudio/en-US/18d73cc3-d9ad-4e53-8f2c-b3a9c0f13d82/how-to-write-pex-fuzz-tests-for-non-generic-collections?forum=vstest
As a reference, here is an example of a contract tested for non-generic collection:
https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/smart-fuzz-for-net/code/src/code/SmartFuzz.UnitTest.NonGenericCollection.cs
This is a project for fuzz testing of non-generic collections: https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/smart-fuzz/
Please be aware that these are advanced topics and you should be familiar with .NET and Pex.
Testing with Pex it is very intuitive and fast, but requires an extensive knowledge about Pex capabilities.
I hope this helps with your research.

A: Microsoft's Pex is quite limited in the capabilities for testing non-generic collections like ArrayList, especially as it doesn't understand the differences between List<T> and ArrayList. 
You may need to consider creating your own version of ArrayList that you can test thoroughly with Pex. It would need--------------------            if (New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount && Old_arrListContainsX == New_arrListContainsX)
            {
		// an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ For every valid add to list, last index of x remains as old Last index + 1 
                if (Old_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_Ret)
                    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);

		// an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ For every valid add to list, element count remains as old count+1 
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            }
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the element is added into empty list, index of x will be 0 
	    else if (!Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX)
                Assert.AreEqual(0, New_Ret);
        }
    }
}

The generated code is actually wrong because it will fail the test.
Below is the correct code to check that the IndexOf method produces the expected output:

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);
            arrList.Add(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ If the element is added into empty list, index of x will be 0 
            if (Old_arrListCount == 0) 
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(0, New_Ret);
            }
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ For every valid add to list, the element count remains as old count+1 
            else 
            {
                Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount + 1, New_arrListCount);
            }

            // The element should appear at least as many times as you've already added
            Assert.GreaterOrEqual(New_Ret, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}

The above code checks that:
- If the element is added into empty list, IndexOf of element is 0.
- For every valid add to list, the Element count remains the old count + 1.
- IndexOf returns the first index of the given element in the list. The first element has index 0.
- The element appears at least as many times as already present in the list.
This makes the IndexOf method work as expected. 

A: ArrayList has different implementations of IndexOf and Contains based on type. It's likely that you need to add some specific preconditions or specify the PexSettings to make sure PEX can use the correct method.
Also, it seems that ArrayList's--------------------            if (New_Ret < 0) { Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount); }
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ if return value is equal to last index of x, list count remains same
            if (New_Ret == Old_arrListLastIndexOfX) { Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount); }
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ the last index of x is less than list.count
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX < New_arrListCount);
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ return value less than or equal to list.count
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ list contains x
            PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

            // check that if x is in the list, IndexOf returns a non-negative value, and this value is no larger than the count of the list
            PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX ? (New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret < New_arrListCount) : (New_Ret < 0));
        }
    }
}


A: Try adding the following into PexSettings section of your [TestMethod]. 
[PexMethod]
[PexSettings(@"SkipVerification=true")]

This skips the exhaustive coverage part of the Pex test and should make things run a bit faster for unit tests. However, it's not foolproof and it could generate faulty results. 
If the unit test does not pass, then it's advisable to debug what's going on and do proper verification afterwards. 
Also remember to refactor your tests after adjusting the settings, as the testing behavior could change.
You might also want to look into partial mocking to improve the performance which is explained here: https://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/projects/partialmock/ 

A: If you're using Pex for unit testing, there are several areas you might consider improving the performance - 
- In the [PexMethod] attribute you can use PexSettings:@"SkipVerification=true" to skip the more computationally-intensive verification stage.
- If your methods have side effects, try separating them out to other unit tests. This can help speed up testing.
- In your Pex settings, you could use "MaxConstraintSolverTime" to set a limit on the runtime for the constraint solver.
- Consider using PexAssumeUnderTest attribute to provide enough exploration for the Pex fuzzer.
- Try using [PexIgnore] to ignore the methods or properties that are too slow or too unrealistic for Pex to test.
- Consider using PexAllowedCalls to decrease the number of paths that the fuzzer has to explore.
- You could also refactor your code to better reduce test execution times.
- Turn off Pex for some tests by removing the [PexMethod] attribute.
This should help improve testing speed but keep in mind running Pex tests could take longer time than unit tests as it explores all the paths of the method under test.
Remember that Pex is a black box testing tool and focuses on exploring all possible paths in your code. The performance may not always increase as the test coverage increases.

A: 
Why all these methods won't work, even though they seem to be recommended for improving performance?

There are a few reasons as well:


*

*SkipVerification 


While the name might suggest skipping verification, it's instead designed to save time during the exploration phase, which seems to be what you've been using the option for. It doesn't have an effect on the results, but rather the coverage.


*MaxConstraintSolverTime, PexAssumeUnderTest, PexIgnore, PexAllowedCalls 


These are all good ideas in isolation, but they may not work as expected if not used properly, particularly PexIgnore. It would be used on methods or classes that are not worth the computation time to test.


*Refactoring 


Refactoring can be complex and time-consuming. It's typically done before testing and not directly related to performance.
A recommendation would be to--------------------            Assume.That(!(New_Ret < 0));
            Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);

		    
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ The position of an existing item is not negative.
	        Assume.That(Old_arrListContainsX == true && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == Old_arrListCount - 1);
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret >= 0);


            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ The index returned by IndexOf is equal to LastIndexOf value for the same item.
            Assert.AreEqual(New_Ret, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);


            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ The IndexOf value is less than count of the array.
            Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret <= New_arrListCount);
        }
    }
}

//src/ArrayList.Test/ListContractTests/Remove/ArrayListContractTest_RemoveContract.cs
// ReSharper disable once CheckNamespace

using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList)
        {
            Assume.True(arrList.Count > 0);
            object item = arrList[0];
            int oldCount = arrList.Count;
            arrList.Remove(item);
            int newCount = arrList.Count;
            Assert.IsTrue(newCount <= oldCount);
        }
    }
}

//src/ArrayList.Test/ListContractTests/Sort/ArrayListContractTest_SortContract.cs
// ReSharper disable once CheckNamespace

using System;
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Validation;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void SortContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList)
        {
            Assume.True(arrList.Count > 0);
            arrList.Sort();
            for (int i = 0; i < arrList.Count - 1; i++)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(arrList[i] is IComparable && ((IComparable)arrList[i]).CompareTo(arrList[i + 1]) <= 0);
            }
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void SortContract_Comparer([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, IComparer comparer)
        {
            Assume.True(arrList.Count > 0);
            arrList.Sort(comparer);
            for (int i = 0; i < arrList.Count - 1; i++)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(comparer.Compare(arrList[i], arrList[i + 1]) <= 0);
            }
        }
    }
}

//src/ArrayList.Test/ListContractTests/ToArray/ArrayListContractTest_ToArrayContract.cs
// ReSharper disable once CheckNamespace

using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void ToArrayContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList)
        {
            object[] array = arrList.ToArray();

            // Assert the Count
            PexAssert.AreEqual(arrList.Count, array.Length);

            // Assert the item in arrList - we assume that 'arrList' does not contain duplicate items
            for (int i = 0; i < array.Length; i++)
            {
                PexAssert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(array[i]));
            }
        }
    }
}

