Test Code:
[TestMethod]
public void TestArrayListIndex([PexGeneratedByRef]ArrayList arrList, [PexGeneratedByRef]int x)
{
   arrList.Add(x); //Just to make sure there is at least one occurrence of x in arrList
   Contract.Requires(arrList.Contains(x)); //Ensuring the initial condition
   ArrayListContractTest t = new ArrayListContractTest();
   t.PUT_IndexOfContract(arrList, x);
}


A: The problem is that Pex randomly generates the test data for your test methods and your ArrayListContractTest only has a constructor that takes no parameters. This is why your code is not working, since you have a dependency on the actual contents of the ArrayList you pass to your method for the PUT_IndexOfContract.
To fix this problem, you should:


*

*Rename the constructor in ArrayListContractTest to take an ArrayList as a parameter and assign it to a private field.

*Add a property that returns the list for verification in your test.


Here is the ArrayListContractTest class that solves the problem:
public partial class ArrayListContractTest
{
    private ArrayList arrList;

    public ArrayListContractTest(ArrayList arrList)
    {
        this.arrList = arrList;
    }

    public ArrayList ArrList 
    {
        get { return this.arrList; }
    }

    // your other code...
}

And here is how you can call your contract test in Pex:
[PexMethod]
[PexAssembly]
public void TestArrayListIndex([PexGeneratedByRef]ArrayList arrList, [PexGeneratedByRef]int x)
{
    arrList.Add(x);

    ArrayListContractTest t = new ArrayListContractTest(arrList);
    t.PUT_IndexOfContract(t.ArrList, x);
}

The PexAssembly attribute will tell Pex to include this method as a test case in the automated test execution. The PexGeneratedByRef attribute tells Pex to generate one test case per input parameter. For ArrayList type, Pex will make many test cases with input data that you didn't expected if you used PexInvariant instead of PexGeneratedByRef. 
This approach will ensure the contents of the ArrayList are preserved for verification inside ArrayListContractTest.
Refer to the Pex documentation for more details on generating test cases and the different attribute values.
Edit: As of .NET framework 4.0, the ArrayList class is obsolete. It is advised to use List<T> instead which provides better type safety and performance.
Q: C++: What's the best way to remove the highest value in an array? Here is a program I wrote to remove an element of the highest value in an array. Here, a1 is the array,  n is the number of elements in a1, and maxElement is the variable holding the maximum value in a1.
for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
    if(a1[j] == maxElement) {
        for(int k = j; k < n-1; k++) {
            a1[k] = a1[k+1];
        }
        n--;
    }
}

This program removes all instances of the highest value in the array. However, it seems to have a problem. Some elements of the array may be lost if two maximum values are next to each other. For example, if a1 is {1, 5, 9, 5, 2}, after running the program, a1 becomes {1, 5, 2}. The higher value '9' is missing from the array.
How can I fix this problem?

A: What you're missing is all the way from the 0th to largest element index from your previous iterations. 
Here is the proper solution:
// Find the largest element's index from array
int maxIndex = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)
    if (a1[i] > a1[maxIndex])
        maxIndex = i;
--------------------